title: How challenging should art be?
pid: b5b49a14-b391-4db8-9e06-b50da7d25831
body: |2-
  Maybe what felt inauthentic about the long anti-(nukes? war?) poem you read was that it rails against someone faceless who's not listening. It claims purity for the speaker and the listener, which feels impotent.

  Things like war and environmental depredation can be usefully personalized. They are the result not only of unreachable powerful apparently-evil people, but also and more importantly of a mass of ordinary people who use weapons and ruin the world to feed their families. Ignorance of those harmed sooths the harmer's conscience, and society invests a lot into that ignorance, and the best art attacks it.

  Art can only change the people who give it their attention, but it doesn't have to reach the pinnacles of power to make a difference. Most humans seem guilty of some kind of convenient, violence-enabling othering. Eating meat, for instance, isn't so unlike opportunistic war.
